From 142f02c0edf7e51c0d0eac3be88f074cac35340b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrea Campanella <emuboy85@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 13:45:49 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] block: partitions: Read the APA format of the PlayStation 2
 hard disk

The system software of the PlayStation 2, uLaunchELF and OPL keep the
hard disk in the APA format: a ring of partition headers, each in the
first two sectors of its segment. Linux could not read it, so a disk
for Linux had to lose its APA table, and with it the partitions of the
console.

The new reader walks the ring from sector 0 and gives one partition for
each main segment of a used APA partition. The Linux partition starts
4 MiB into the segment, after the area that APA keeps for the header
(PFS starts there too), so a Linux file system never writes over a
header. Sub segments are not included: their own headers sit inside
the range they would add. The APA name becomes the partition name
(PARTNAME), so user space finds a partition by name.

The reader comes before EFI and MS-DOS, because ps2sdk can write a
protective MBR into the first APA sector.

Upstream-Status: Pending
